= Problem Statement (Track 3 — broad framing)
Malaysia is accelerating its transition to Electric Vehicles (EVs), driven by government policy incentives, expanding charging infrastructure, and growing private sector investment. As EV adoption gains momentum, it is transforming not only mobility but also the insurance, risk management, and broader mobility ecosystem.

The ecosystem, however, is growing faster than its support structures. Three structural constraints threaten the long-term resilience, sustainability, and scalability of Malaysia's EV future:

1. *Repair cost inflation.* Battery packs cost RM30,000–80,000+ to replace (up to RM106,000 depending on brand); few EV-certified workshops exist; parts are largely OEM-controlled. Repairs skew toward total loss, and EV premiums run 10–30% above ICE.
2. *Charging infrastructure gaps.* The 10,000-charger target was missed (5,839 points as of 31 Mar 2026 vs 2025 deadline); coverage is thin on the East Coast and in East Malaysia; uptime is unreliable; home/condo installation remains costly (RM4,000–10,000).
3. *Emerging systemic risks.* Flood exposure (Special Perils gaps on battery claims), battery fire incidents rising (27 EV/hybrid fires since 2023, JBPM), grid capacity limits, cybersecurity, and zero claims-history data pool. Motor insurance already runs at a 102.2% combined ratio — the industry has no margin for error.

No single actor owns these risks. Insurers, CPOs, OEMs, and policymakers each optimise their own silo. The core Track 3 question: *how do we coordinate risk management across the ecosystem so that EV adoption is financially sustainable, resilient, and scalable?*

= Findings on EVs in Malaysian landscape
== Key facts
*Market size*
- 278 EVs (2021) → 13,000+ (2023) → 20,000+ (2024) → 44,813 registered (end-2025) → ~50,000+ cumulative projected end-2026
- EV+hybrid = 8.7% of 2025 new car sales; EV share of new registrations jumped 3.6% → 7.8% (1H2025)
- Market leaders: BYD (8,570 units 2024), Tesla (5,137), BMW, Mercedes, GWM/Ora
- MAA warns Malaysia losing EV race to SEA neighbours (sub-4% EV sales vs Thailand/Vietnam, May 2026)
- Government targets: xEV 20% of TIV by 2030, 50% by 2040, 80% by 2050
- Charging market volume: 274k units (2025) → 893k forecast (2030), CAGR 26.6%

*Insurance & claims economics*
- Motor GWP RM5.0b → RM5.3b (1H2024 → 1H2025, +5.7%), but combined ratio 102.2% — underwriting loss (motor = 42.8% of total industry premiums)
- General insurance industry GWP RM24.2b in 2025 (+4.8%); underwriting profit +32% 1H2025 driven by non-motor lines
- EV premiums typically 10–30% higher than comparable ICE; in open markets (UK) ~2x
- BNM semi-regulated pricing framework has kept Malaysian EV premiums stable — for now
- Battery replacement RM11,000–106,000 by brand; battery = large share of vehicle value → moderate damage can trigger total loss
- UK benchmark (Thatcham): BEV claims 25.5% costlier, repairs 14% slower

*Incentives*
- CBU EV import duty + excise + sales tax exemption: until 31 Dec 2025 (expired — shifted to localization push)
- CKD locally assembled EVs: exempt until 31 Dec 2027
- Road tax exemption for EVs until 2027; kW-based road tax structure from Jan 2026
- Income tax relief RM2,500/yr for home charging expenses (till 2027 assessment year)
- MARiiCas rebate up to RM2,400 for electric motorcycles
- New: RM200,000 minimum CIF for CBU EVs from July 2026 — pushes budget Chinese EVs to local assembly or premium pricing; raises sums insured and premiums
- 2026 shift: government pivoting from imported EVs to local assembly/ecosystem (Perodua National EV Project: 62 local suppliers, 300+ engineers; Proton: 40 local firms)

*Charging infrastructure*
- 5,624 public chargers (Mar 2026, The Star) / 5,839 (MEVnet, 31 Mar 2026) / 6,416 (MITI, 31 May 2026 — 2,143 DC, 4,273 AC) — official numbers inconsistent
- 10,000-by-2025 target missed, pushed to Q3 2026 (some sources say 2028)
- GPP EVCB guidelines cut approval time to as fast as 14 working days; fire blankets mandated at charging bays
- Klang Valley leads (~650 points), Penang 180, JB 120; East Coast & East Malaysia weak
- Home install RM4,000–10,000 (charger + electrician + TNB 3-phase upgrade RM3,000–6,000 if needed)
- Oversight: NEVTF (MITI) + NEVSC (DPM Fadillah Yusof)

*Charging costs (2026)*
- Home AC 7kW: RM0.50–0.60/kWh → full 50kWh charge RM25–30 (ToU off-peak ~41.83 sen/kWh)
- Destination AC 11–22kW: RM0.60–0.80 → RM30–40
- DC 50kW: RM0.90–1.20 → RM45–60
- Ultra-fast 150–350kW: RM1.40–1.80 → RM70–90
- Running cost: RM0.08–0.12/km vs RM0.20–0.25 petrol — 60–70% cheaper

== Recent news & insights (as of 12 Aug 2026)
*Insurance industry readiness (PIAM's own admissions)*
- PIAM: "the motor insurance industry may not be ready" for EV boom — examining whether private workshops can adapt to EV repairs, how battery life coverage is managed, and whether JPJ recognises EV standards (Oct 2025)
- People switching ICE → EV have *higher risk* of being involved in an accident; EV accidents "far more complex and costly," sometimes unrepairable entirely (PIAM, May 2026)
- Malaysia lacks a mature battery repair ecosystem — increases claims costs on serious accidents (PIAM, Oct 2025)
- JPJ engineering department is setting standards/guidelines for independent repair workshops — a live policy conversation we can plug into
- PIAM supported MITI's initiative for Malaysian Standard — Motor Vehicle Aftermarket Smash Repair Requirements (Dec 2023)
- PIAM warns of rogue tow truck operators steering victims to workshops that inflate prices for commissions — claims-cost leakage (Sep 2025)

*EV fires & safety*
- 27 EV/hybrid fires 2023–Jul 2025 (JBPM): 13 EV, 14 hybrid; ~10/year, rising trend; Selangor highest
- Causes: short circuits, charging faults, BMS failures, overcharging → thermal runaway
- EV fires rare relative to ICE: 1 per 100,000 EVs vs 1,530 per 100,000 petrol vehicles — but harder to extinguish
- JBPM calls EV fires "biggest challenge"; 318 fire blankets acquired; cooling agents/immersion containers under investigation
- ZEVA: no electric shock incidents at charging stations during floods so far; most stations meet ST/IP safety standards

*Floods & climate*
- Flash floods rising in Klang Valley; flood (Special Perils) coverage not automatic in motor policies
- Gap: policy may cover flood damage but under-compensate high-cost battery — a ~RM50,000 battery protection gap identified by industry
- TNB reassures EV/charging safety in floods (auto cut-off, sealed batteries), monitors flood-prone areas in real time

*Policy & market structure*
- RM200,000 CIF floor for CBU EVs (Jul 2026) — MAA warns phased approach needed; MITI insists rules target local industry development, not just protecting Proton/Perodua
- Malaysia Day 2026 celebrations; haze season affecting Sarawak (API 170) — weather risk is live
- Puspakom launched AI undercarriage scanner (Aug 2026): inspection 10 min → 1 min — inspection modernisation signals
- Financial groups reigniting race for insurance deals (Jul 2026); insurance sector "to hold firm in 2H" — capital entering the space

*Data inconsistencies to flag in our analysis*
- Charger counts: 5,624 (Star) vs 5,839 (MEVnet) vs 6,416 (MITI) — no single source of truth
- EV share stats: 7.8% of new registrations (PIAM 1H2025) vs sub-4% (MAA May 2026) vs 8.7% EV+hybrid (PIAM 2025) — definitions differ (BEV vs xEV)
- These gaps themselves are evidence of the ecosystem's data-immaturity problem

= Global benchmarks: what other markets solved (and didn't)
No country has fully solved the EV insurance/ecosystem problem — each solved one slice. The collective picture points to four transferable pillars.

