== Four pillars Malaysia can collectively copy
1. *Data platforms* — China's CAA-CBIT exchange and the EU Battery Passport show the way: a national EV claims + battery-health data pool, mandated through BNM's semi-regulated pricing framework, gives insurers pricing power we currently lack.
2. *Repair-friendly design* — UK Thatcham, Chinese modular batteries, and ICA Australia converge on the same ask: design EVs to be repairable. Malaysia's CKD push (Proton eMas, Perodua) is the one-time window to embed this before mass adoption — the late-mover advantage.
3. *Independent repairer access* — the EU Data Act and Right to Repair Directive grant workshops parts and diagnostics; JPJ's in-progress repair workshop standards are Malaysia's entry point. Data access breaks the OEM/4S repair monopoly that inflates bills in every market.
4. *Segmented repairability pricing* — Norway (profitability-first), the UK (insurance-group ratings), and China (repairability-based reform) all price by model risk profile. Malaysia can skip blanket EV-vs-ICE pricing entirely.

== Anti-patterns to avoid
- *China's sequencing*: subsidies and mass adoption arrived before insurance data infrastructure — a decade of underwriting losses followed. Malaysia can build the data layer first.
- *Norway's scale trap*: a market can be too small to influence OEM design. Malaysia must use policy leverage (CKD incentives, import approval) rather than market gravity.
- *UK's affordability deterrence*: premium shock suppresses uptake (UK consumers cite premiums as switch deterrent). Containing repair costs now protects the 20%-by-2030 xEV target.

